<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Holowczyn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1708">1708</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Holowczyn · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Holowczyn was fought in 1708 in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII of Sweden</a> defeated a Russian army in what was one of his last major victories. The campaign continued into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and ended in disaster at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Poltava</a> the following year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-holowczyn.jpg" alt="Battle of Holowczyn" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: B. Adlerfeld · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Holowczyn_1708_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Having dealt with Saxony-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> turned on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Rather than the easier route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grodno">Grodno</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>, he chose a straighter but harder one across the Berezina and Drut rivers, and in June 1708 moved his army from its winter camp near Radoszkowice through bad roads and weather. Unsure of his intentions, and with Peter I away, the Russian command, in which Field Marshal Boris Sheremetev had to contend with the rivalry of Alexander Menshikov, decided to hold a line by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> and moved its army piecemeal to the marshy Vabich, a tributary of the Drut.</p><p>The main Russian force stood around Vasilki, behind fortified bridges, while General Anikita Repnin, in his first independent command, held a long, thin and half-fortified line three kilometres to the southeast. Between the two camps lay marsh that could not be fortified, and the Russians had no overall commander and little contact with each other. Charles saw the gap and planned a night attack through it to split the enemy in two.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>At midnight on 4 July the Swedes moved towards the river with fascines, leaving behind pontoon bridges that heavy rain had made too heavy to carry. The Russian alarm was raised at 02:30, when Swedish artillery opened fire, and Charles led the crossing himself, wading in front of his men. Repnin saw the danger of a wedge between the Russian positions and ordered his men north towards Sheremetev, but Swedish battalions kept the two forces apart, and Repnin was driven east and south while Swedish cavalry scattered the Russian horse.</p><p>Sheremetev's forces, still across the river from Holowczyn, had been on full alert for hours in the belief that the attack on Repnin was a feint. Sheremetev moved against the almost undefended Swedish camp to the west, but when news of Repnin's setback reached him he began retreating towards Shklov on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> rather than wait for an attack on his rear. Only 9,000 Russians took part in the battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The victory gave the Swedes a line along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> and a base around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mogilev">Mogilev</a>, but so many Russians escaped that it was not a decisive strategic victory. Repnin and Heinrich von der Goltz were tried for the failures of the Russian command and later released. Official Swedish and Russian reports gave 1,293 Swedes and 1,655 Russians killed and wounded, while each side's other sources claimed far higher losses for the enemy. It was reportedly Charles's favourite victory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Holowczyn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Charles XII (r. 1697–1718) was the high point of personal Swedish generalship: a commander of great tactical skill whose victories at Narva (1700) and <b>Holowczyn</b> (1708) against numerically superior opponents rank among the finest tactical performances of the early eighteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
